## §2. Change 1 — START.oot: in-flight state pointer

  Goal: leave a one-line breadcrumb on the agent entry gate so a future
  agent reading START.oot (the very first board per START.oot:25) can
  find the durable record of the 2026-08-19 in-flight work without
  globbing audit/.

  Current state of START.oot (read 2026-08-19):
    Total lines: 92
    Last 5 lines (START.oot:88-92):
      88  No goal and axiom means no product edit.
      89
      90  Decide at most 5 work items per turn. Prefer one clear ship.
      91  Then run WORKFLOW.oot (write map, then OODA tempo). Respect
         lane ownership in ROADMAP.oot.
      92    Residual ROADMAP tracks are not this week's work unless the
         owner named the row.
    Layout: 5 top-level sections (## 0..## 4). The 9-board index lives
    in §1 (START.oot:25-33). Reading priority lives in §3 (START.oot:62).
    Pre-modification gate lives in §4 (START.oot:82-92).
    No section number 5 exists; the slot is free.

  Proposed addition (insert after START.oot:92, becomes lines 93-98):

    ## 5. In-Flight State Pointer (2026-08-19)

      Active repair pass:
        audit/process_board_update_spec.oot — durable spec for board edits
        audit/swarm_state_2026_08_19.oot   — lane ownership snapshot
        audit/session_rollup_2026_08_19.oot — turn-level rollup

  Why:
    §1 already names 9 boards. A future agent reading START.oot cold
    should also see the active audit/ anchors, not have to glob them.
    The pointer names three durable files so the entry gate does not
    go stale when this session ends.

  Rollback (one command, reverts exactly):
    Open START.oot. Delete lines 93-98 (the new section 5). Save.
    The file returns to 92 lines, identical to its pre-edit content.

  Line budget after the change:
    92 + 6 = 98 lines. Cap is 256. Headroom: 158 lines. Safe.

## §3. Change 2 — WORKFLOW.oot: line-cap watch + pointer

  Goal: (a) document that WORKFLOW.oot is at 246 of 256 lines so future
  edits treat it as 1-2 line patches, and (b) add a one-line pointer
  to the audit/ spec that records the 2026-08-19 in-flight work.

  Current state of WORKFLOW.oot (read 2026-08-19):
    Total lines: 246
    Last 5 lines (WORKFLOW.oot:242-246):
      242  9. Never copy CLI onto compiler. Identity is `grep -a` only.
          No Cargo. No ELF transmutation.
      243
      244  Also see:
      245    - `openOODA/RULES.oot` — Core repository laws and pure .oo
          standards
      246    - `openOODA/SECURITY.oot` — Permission token taxonomy and
          capability matrix
    Layout: 6 top-level sections (## 0..## 5). Section 5 is "Bug Lore"
    (WORKFLOW.oot:222). The "Also see:" tail block is at WORKFLOW.oot:243-248
    in spirit, but only 3 reference lines (244-246) plus the heading.

  Proposed addition (insert after WORKFLOW.oot:246, becomes lines 247-249):

    Line-cap watch: 246 of 256 lines. Treat future edits as 1-2 line
    patches. Past 250, split into door + companion (mirror the
    audit/library_status.oot pattern).
    Active repair pass pointer: see audit/process_board_update_spec.oot
    for the 2026-08-19 in-flight sub-agent state.

  Why:
    (a) WORKFLOW.oot has only 10 lines of headroom. Without a watch
    note, a future agent could add 11 lines, fail FORMAT.oot:91, and
    not realize why. The note names the cap and the safety valve.
    (b) The pointer matches the START.oot pointer so a future agent
    who reads WORKFLOW.oot (the second board per START.oot:69) can find
    the same audit/ anchors without globbing.

  Rollback (one command, reverts exactly):
    Open WORKFLOW.oot. Delete lines 247-249 (the new tail block).
    Save. The file returns to 246 lines, identical to its pre-edit
    content.

  Line budget after the change:
    246 + 3 = 249 lines. Cap is 256. Headroom: 7 lines. Safe but tight.
    If any concurrent edit lands on the same day, the 7-line headroom
    may be consumed. See §4.

## §4. Risk note: WORKFLOW.oot is at 246 of 256 lines

  The 256-line cap is law (FORMAT.oot:91, RULES 1.7). WORKFLOW.oot is
  at 246 lines today. After the change in §3, it sits at 249 — 7 lines
  under cap.

  Risk:
    Any other 2026-08-19 edit that adds 8 or more lines to WORKFLOW.oot
    will violate the cap. The two most likely offenders are:
      i.  Another sub-agent adding its own pointer (5+ lines).
      ii. A future agent merging process-board conventions that the
          current spec does not anticipate.

  Mitigation already in place:
    The line-cap watch note (§3) names the cap and the 1-2 line patch
    rule, so any agent reading the file post-edit sees the constraint.

  Safety valve (the library_status pattern):
    If a future edit must add more than the 7-line headroom allows,
    split WORKFLOW.oot into a door and a companion. The pattern is
    already proven:
      audit/library_status.oot        — 44 lines, door with one-line
                                         pointer to 22 siblings
      audit/library_status_tail_*.oot — 17 sibling files, each holds
                                         a section of the data
    Concretely, the split would be:
      openOODA/WORKFLOW.oot             — §0..§2 (write map, tempo,
                                          swarm). Door only.
      openOODA/WORKFLOW_lore.oot        — §3..§5 (hostile probing,
                                          tokenomics, bug lore).
                                          Companion.
    After the split, WORKFLOW.oot lands at roughly 180 lines and the
    companion at roughly 90 lines, both well under cap. The door keeps
    a one-line pointer to the companion, matching the library_status
    pattern.

  Why not split today:
    This repair pass only adds 3 lines. Splitting now is a refactor
    outside the scope of the 2026-08-19 ask and risks unrelated board
    drift. Split when the cap actually bites, not preemptively.

## §5. Acceptance test

  After both edits are applied, the following must hold:

    1. wc -l openOODA/START.oot = 98       (was 92, +6)
    2. wc -l openOODA/WORKFLOW.oot = 249   (was 246, +3)
    3. Both files <= 256 lines             (FORMAT.oot:91)
    4. START.oot §5 exists, lines 93-98, and reads verbatim as the
       "Proposed addition" block in §2 of this spec.
    5. WORKFLOW.oot lines 247-249 exist, and read verbatim as the
       "Proposed addition" block in §3 of this spec.
    6. openOODA/scripts/dinner_lint.oo passes with no new violations
       (it already enforces 256-line cap, forbidden extensions, and
       Academy headers per FORMAT.oot:144-148).
    7. No other board file is touched (md5sum of the other 7 boards
       is unchanged).
    8. The audit/ siblings (audit/library_status.oot, audit/swarm_state_
       2026_08_19.oot, audit/session_rollup_2026_08_19.oot) are not
       modified by this edit.

  Owner of acceptance run: the human operator. The spec author does
  not run dinner_lint; the spec author only writes the durable change
  record and trusts the executor to run the lock.

